At \(n=8\), the full-count rule \(t_8\) has maximum risk \(U_8\) over all \(165\) response-type states, with equality only at \((2,0,0,6)\) and \((6,0,0,2)\).  The weights defining \(\pi_8^{\mathrm{CR}}\) are positive and sum to one, and its Bayes risk after observing \((X_1,X_0)\) is
\[
 C_8=\frac{23126566130927189325821140923541183}
 {353617236146857430307249306610000000}.
\]
The weights defining \(\pi_8^W\) are positive and sum to one, and its Bayes risk after observing \(W\) is \(L_8\).  Exact rational comparison yields
\[
 C_8\leq\rho_8^{\mathrm{CR}}\leq U_8
 =\frac{457801172643}{7000000000000}
 <\frac{6541}{100000}<\frac{6578}{100000}
 <\frac{224641744872980943629642927781}
 {3414537001722982784239360000000}
 =L_8\leq\rho_8^W.
\]
Moreover \(C_8<U_8\), so these certificates do not determine the exact value of \(\rho_8^{\mathrm{CR}}\).  In particular, \(\rho_8^{\mathrm{CR}}<\rho_8^W\).
